  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "sanitize",
    "file": "harfbuzz/src/hb-ot-kern-table.hh",
    "description": "`KernSubTableFormat3::sanitize` unconditionally returns true at line 81, leaving its actual structural/range validation unreachable. Consequently, a format-3 `kern` subtable can advertise arrays much larger than the bytes actually present while still passing table sanitization. During shaping, `KernSubTableFormat3::get_kerning` derives `leftClass`, `rightClass`, and `kernIndex` pointers from attacker-controlled counts and immediately reads `leftClass[left]`; in this PoV that pointer is already far beyond the font blob, causing the heap-buffer-overflow in `BEInt::operator unsigned char()`. The root cause is the disabled format-3 sanitizer, not the endian-integer accessor where the invalid read manifests.",
    "conditions": "A crafted SFNT must expose a legacy OpenType `kern` table with at least one horizontal format-3 subtable so shaping dispatches it. The PoV places the `kern` table at file offset 16 and declares a 16-byte subtable, but its format-3 fields claim `glyphCount = 0xff20` and `kernValueCount = 0xff` despite the blob containing no corresponding arrays. Outer sanitization accepts the declared 16-byte subtable and format-3 sanitization accepts it unconditionally. Shaping the harness's fixed text produces adjacent glyph IDs (notably glyph 0 with this minimal font), and `get_kerning` computes `leftClass` after 255 absent FWORD values and reads `leftClass[0]` outside the allocation. More generally, the encoded counts must make one of the derived format-3 arrays extend beyond the actual subtable/blob and kerning must be requested for at least one glyph pair."
  },
